<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jublains</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Noviodunum Diablintum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jublains is a village in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mayenne">Mayenne</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, on the site of Noviodunum Diablintum, the Roman capital of the Gallic Diablintes. Its remains include a temple, baths, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and an unusual late Roman fortified building, probably a store for military supplies. The town declined in late antiquity, when its territory was absorbed into that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/le-mans">Le Mans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jublains.jpg" alt="Jublains" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Pascal Radigue · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jublains_fontaine_d%C3%A9tail.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
